<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joachim Murat</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1767">1767</a>–<a href="/years/1815">1815</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joachim Murat was a French cavalry commander and Marshal of the Empire who married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s sister Caroline. Napoleon made him Grand Duke of Berg in 1806 and King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> in 1808, where he ruled as Joachim Napoleon and introduced French reforms. He abandoned Napoleon in 1814, rejoined him in 1815 and was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> at Tolentino. Captured during an attempt to regain Naples, he was shot in 1815.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joachim-murat.jpg" alt="Joachim Murat" width="320" height="389" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: François Gérard · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Murat2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Joachim Murat was born on 25 March 1767 at La Bastide-Fortunière in Guyenne, in the present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Lot, the youngest of six surviving children. His father, Pierre Murat-Jordy, was an affluent yeoman, innkeeper and postmaster who also served as steward for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-maurice-de-talleyrand">Talleyrand</a> family. His parents intended him for the church, and he studied at the College of Saint-Michel at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cahors">Cahors</a> and then at the Lazarist seminary in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>. In February 1787 he left the seminary, either expelled or, as others say, having run away with a girl, and enlisted in a cavalry regiment, the Chasseurs des <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardennes">Ardennes</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>An ardent Republican, Murat served in the revolutionary army and, at the royalist rising of 13 Vendémiaire in October 1795, brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> of the Camp des Sablons to the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, which let General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> save the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">National Convention</a>. He went with Bonaparte to northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1796, commanded the cavalry in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, where he led the charge that broke the Ottoman line at Abukir on 25 July 1799, and was decisive in the coup of 18 Brumaire. He married Caroline Bonaparte in a civil ceremony on 20 January 1800.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> made him a Marshal of the Empire on 18 May 1804 and Admiral of the Empire in 1805. He fought at Ulm, Austerlitz, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> and Eylau, where he led a massed cavalry charge against the Russians.</p><p>Murat was Grand Duke of Berg and Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a> from 15 March 1806 and King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> from 1 August 1808. In the Russian campaign of 1812 he was the best cavalry commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grande-armee">Grande Armée</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> and Borodino, though he showed little concern for the horses, which starved and tired on the long marches. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leipzig">Battle of Leipzig</a> he reached a secret agreement with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> to save his throne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> returned from exile, Murat changed sides again, and on 15 March 1815 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. His army of about 45,000 invaded the Papal States and Tuscany, but he was defeated at Occhiobello and decisively at Tolentino on 2 and 3 May, and returned to Naples on 18 May. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a> he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a> and tried to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a> with about 200 men. He was captured, sentenced to death and shot at Pizzo Calabro on 13 October 1815. Murat and Caroline had four children.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Joachim Murat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1806 Napoleon's armies drove Ferdinand back to Sicily, where he reigned under British protection, while the mainland became a French satellite under Joseph Bonaparte, who abolished feudalism, and from 1808 under <b>Joachim Murat</b>, who modernised the administration and law.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Driven out in the Napoleonic years, Ferdinand kept some of the reforms of Kings Joseph and <b>Joachim Murat</b> to the civil, penal and commercial codes and extended them to Sicily, but inherited brigandage and the forcible occupation of lands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-two-sicilies">Kingdom of the Two Sicilies · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1806 Napoleon made Berg a grand duchy for his brother-in-law <b>Joachim Murat</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-berg">Duchy of Berg</a></li><li><blockquote>He combined it with Cleves and other lands and gave it to his brother-in-law <b>Joachim Murat</b> as the Grand Duchy of Berg, with Düsseldorf as capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-duchy-of-berg">Grand Duchy of Berg</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1808 he became king of Spain and was succeeded by <b>Joachim Murat</b>, Napoleon's brother-in-law, who introduced the Napoleonic Code, built roads and fought brigandage in Calabria.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-naples-1806-1815">Kingdom of Naples (1806–1815)</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1810 he was elected crown prince of Sweden and gave up the principality, which Napoleon in 1812 granted to Lucien Murat, son of King <b>Joachim Murat</b> of the Kingdom of Naples (1806–1815).</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-pontecorvo">Principality of Pontecorvo</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Labastide-Murat, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Pizzo, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.65,1.57&amp;year=1798" aria-label="Labastide-Murat on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4587 585 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Joachim Murat"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5037 855h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5037 855h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.65,1.57&amp;year=1798">What was here in 1798? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
